Template Argument Deduction
-> Process of deducing the types
-> From the function Argument each argument type is deduced
-> the type argument deduction should lead to same type
-> type conversion are not performed during deduction
-> After deduction template is instantiated
-> override deduction by specific type in template argument list can be performed by 
teplate_function_name<type>(arg1,arg2,...)

Template instatiation -> compiler generates a concrete function from the template
-> for each unique set of template arguments a new function is generated
    Occurs implicitly when 
    -> A function template is invoked
    -> taking address of a function template
    -> using explicit instantiation
    -> creating explicit instantiation 


Example:
#include <iostream>                 
template<typename T>
T Sum(T a, T b) {
    std::cout << "Template function called with type: " << typeid(T).name() << std::endl;
    return a + b;
}

template char Sum(char a, char b); // Explicit instantiation for char type

int main(){
    int a=5, b=10;
    std::cout << "Sum =" << Sum(a,b) << std::endl; // T is deduced to int

    std::cout << "Sum =" << Sum(static_cast<float>(3), 4.5f) << std::endl; // T is deduced to float

    std::cout << "Sum =" << Sum<double>(3.5f, 4.5f) << std::endl; // T is explicitly set to double

    int (*funcPtr)(int, int) = Sum; // T is deduced to int for function pointer
    std::cout << "Sum via function pointer =" << funcPtr(7, 8) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}

